%description:

RFC 2131 -- a client that finds the address of the DHCPACK already in use sends a DHCPDECLINE and
starts again.

Checks RFC2131-DECL-1 (should), DECL-2 (must), DECL-5 (description), DECL-6 (must and must not)
and DECL-7 (must) from doc/project/evidence/standard/rfc2131/catalog.md. The English procedure is
in doc/project/evidence/protocol/dhcp/checks/decline.md#duplicate-address-declined; the program
steps below map onto its expected observations. Mapping of the mockup with an occupant:
server S = server, client C = hostA, host O = hostO.

hostO holds 192.168.1.100 from the start, which is the first address the server gives out, and it
answers an address resolution request for it. The client's own interface carries no address before
DHCP runs, so the scenario does not configure it: DhcpClient refuses to start on an interface that
already has one. This is the one test of the suite that resolves
addresses by traffic on the link and not without it, because the address resolution request is the
stimulus.

Defect: DhcpClient never probes the address of the DHCPACK. handleDhcpAck
records the lease and binds the address, and nothing in it sends an address resolution request or
waits for an answer to one (src/inet/applications/dhcp/DhcpClient.cc, handleDhcpAck and
bindLease). RFC 2131 section 4.4.1 makes the probe a SHOULD: "The client SHOULD perform a check on
the suggested address to ensure that the address is not already in use." So the client cannot
learn of the conflict, step 1 misses its deadline, and the whole of the DHCPDECLINE mechanism stays
unreached.

The failure does not tell whether the client would obey RFC2131-DECL-2 if it ever found a
conflict; it tells that it cannot find one. DhcpClient does have a sendDecline function, at :635,
and nothing calls it. The results record both halves; see results.md.

This test FAILS, and the failure is deliberately NOT declared expected. It is an untestable
claim: the model claims the DHCPDECLINE and the check cannot reach it.

The claim is code. DhcpClient::sendDecline is a complete function that builds the message
(src/inet/applications/dhcp/DhcpClient.cc:632, declared at DhcpClient.h:115), so the model claims
observations 5 to 7. What is missing is the trigger: the probe of observation 2 is a commented-out
block at :315 to 320, so the client never learns of a conflict and nothing can provoke a
DHCPDECLINE. No scenario and no crafted message can supply the trigger, because it would have to
come from inside the client.

So the test fails and declares nothing. A half-written mechanism is a claim -- "not finished" is
not "not claimed" -- and the missing part is named here rather than left in a note that nothing
checks. See results.md.

%includes:
#include "ProtocolTest.h"
#include "DhcpChecks.h"
#include "inet/linklayer/common/MacAddress.h"
#include "inet/networklayer/arp/ipv4/ArpPacket_m.h"

%global:

using namespace inet;
using namespace inet::protocoltest;

static Ipv4Address grantedAddress;

// True when the frame is an address resolution request for this address, with the sender
// protocol address 0.0.0.0 as RFC 2131 section 4.4.1 demands.
static bool isProbeFor(const Packet *frame, const Ipv4Address& address)
{
    auto arp = findChunk<ArpPacket>(frame);
    return arp != nullptr && arp->getOpcode() == ARP_REQUEST
           && arp->getDestIpAddress() == address
           && arp->getSrcIpAddress().isUnspecified();
}

Define_ProtocolTestProgram()
{
    grantedAddress = Ipv4Address();
    return ProtocolTest("rfc2131_duplicate_address_declined")
        // Expected observation 1: the client gets a DHCPACK for the address hostO holds.
        .once(on("hostA.eth[0].mac").signal("packetReceivedFromLower")
                  .match([](const MatchContext& c) {
                      auto dhcp = dhcpOf(c.event.packet);
                      if (dhcp == nullptr || dhcp->getOptions().getMessageType() != DHCPACK)
                          return false;
                      grantedAddress = dhcp->getYiaddr();
                      return quad(grantedAddress) == "192.168.1.100";
                  })
                  .describe("a DHCPACK reaching the client whose yiaddr is 192.168.1.100, the address the occupant holds")
                  .within(5.0))
        // Expected observations 2 and 3: the client probes that address, with sender protocol
        // address 0.0.0.0 and its own hardware address.
        .once(on("hostA.eth[0].mac").signal("packetSentToLower")
                  .match([](const MatchContext& c) { return isProbeFor(c.event.packet, grantedAddress); })
                  .describe("an address resolution request for 192.168.1.100 from the client, with sender protocol address 0.0.0.0")
                  .within(5.0))
        // Expected observation 4: the occupant answers, which tells the client the address is used.
        .once(on("hostO.eth[0].mac").signal("packetSentToLower")
                  .match([](const MatchContext& c) {
                      auto arp = findChunk<ArpPacket>(c.event.packet);
                      return arp != nullptr && arp->getOpcode() == ARP_REPLY
                             && arp->getSrcIpAddress() == grantedAddress;
                  })
                  .describe("the occupant's address resolution reply for 192.168.1.100")
                  .within(1.0))
        // Expected observations 5 to 7: a broadcast DHCPDECLINE naming the address and the server,
        // with no lease time, no parameter request list and ciaddr zero.
        .once(on("hostA.eth[0].mac").signal("packetSentToLower")
                  .match([](const MatchContext& c) {
                      auto dhcp = dhcpOf(c.event.packet);
                      if (dhcp == nullptr || dhcp->getOptions().getMessageType() != DHCPDECLINE)
                          return false;
                      const DhcpOptions& o = dhcp->getOptions();
                      bool broadcast = ipv4Destination(c.event.packet) == "255.255.255.255";  // observation 6
                      bool required = hasOption(dhcp, REQUESTED_IP)
                              && o.getRequestedIp() == grantedAddress
                              && hasOption(dhcp, SERVER_ID);                                  // observation 7
                      bool prohibited = !hasOption(dhcp, LEASE_TIME) && !hasOption(dhcp, PARAM_LIST)
                              && dhcp->getCiaddr().isUnspecified();                           // observation 7
                      return broadcast && required && prohibited;
                  })
                  .describe("a broadcast DHCPDECLINE naming 192.168.1.100 and the server, with no lease time, no parameter request list and ciaddr 0.0.0.0")
                  .within(5.0))
        // Expected observation 8: the client starts again.
        .once(on("hostA.eth[0].mac").signal("packetSentToLower")
                  .match([](const MatchContext& c) { return isDhcp(c.event.packet, DHCPDISCOVER); })
                  .describe("a DHCPDISCOVER after the DHCPDECLINE, which shows that the client started again")
                  .within(25.0))
        // Expected observation 9: the client never uses the address it refused.
        .never(on("hostA.eth[0].mac").signal("packetSentToLower")
                  .match([](const MatchContext& c) {
                      return ipv4Source(c.event.packet) == quad(grantedAddress);
                  })
                  .describe("a frame from the client carrying the refused address as its IP source address")
                  .within(5.0));
}

%activity:
// present only so opp_test compiles the %global above; this module is not in the network.
EV << "rfc2131_duplicate_address_declined compiled\n";

%file: test.ned

import inet.node.ethernet.Eth100M;
import inet.node.ethernet.EthernetSwitch;
import inet.node.inet.StandardHost;
import inet.networklayer.configurator.ipv4.Ipv4NetworkConfigurator;

// The mockup with an occupant: a server, a client, and a host that already holds the address the
// server will offer first.
network Rfc2131OccupantNet
{
    submodules:
        configurator: Ipv4NetworkConfigurator {
            config = xml("<config>" +
                "<interface hosts='server' address='192.168.1.1' netmask='255.255.255.0'/>" +
                "<interface hosts='hostO' address='192.168.1.100' netmask='255.255.255.0'/>" +
                "</config>");
            @display("p=100,50");
        }
        switch: EthernetSwitch { @display("p=350,180"); }
        server: StandardHost { @display("p=250,100"); }
        hostA: StandardHost { @display("p=450,100"); }
        hostO: StandardHost { @display("p=450,260"); }
    connections:
        server.ethg++ <--> Eth100M <--> switch.ethg++;
        hostA.ethg++ <--> Eth100M <--> switch.ethg++;
        hostO.ethg++ <--> Eth100M <--> switch.ethg++;
}

%inifile: omnetpp.ini

[General]
network = Rfc2131OccupantNet
sim-time-limit = 60s
seed-set = 0

# Real address resolution here, and only here: the request the client sends for its new address is
# the stimulus of the check, so it must appear on the link.

*.server.numApps = 1
*.server.app[0].typename = "DhcpServer"
*.server.app[0].interface = "eth0"
*.server.app[0].numReservedAddresses = 100
*.server.app[0].maxNumClients = 100
*.server.app[0].leaseTime = 300s

*.hostA.numApps = 1
*.hostA.app[0].typename = "DhcpClient"
*.hostA.app[0].interface = "eth0"

# The occupant runs no DHCP. It holds 192.168.1.100 and answers for it.
*.hostO.numApps = 0

%contains: stdout
PROTOCOLTEST rfc2131_duplicate_address_declined: PASS
